[{"id": "20260728144302985909_bdcc578c", "type": "Document", "text": "This 
Academia.edu page hosts Jack L. Davis's 2016 Hesperia paper titled \"The Lord 
of the Gold Rings\". The abstract says that in May 2015 a University of 
Cincinnati team discovered a large stone-built tomb of Late Helladic IIA date 
near Tholos Tomb IV at the Palace of Nestor, Pylos. The tomb contained the body
of a single male, estimated at 30-35 years old and dubbed the Griffin Warrior, 
along with hundreds of artifacts made of gold, silver, bronze, ivory, and 
semiprecious stones. Among the gold finds were four signet rings decorated with
Minoan ritual scenes, and the paper says it examines the excavation, describes 
the rings, and considers the significance of their iconography for the 
Mycenaeans who buried them.", "source_id": "20260728144301797710_c7ae1bb1"}, 
