 "Ring of Nestor" Pylos gold signet ring Griffin Warrior tomb iconography date 
                       provenance -site:en.wikipedia.org                       

 • "The Lord of the Gold Rings: The Griffin Warrior of Pylos" [1]              
 • "Jack L Davis" ... "2016, Hesperia" [1]                                     
 • "In May 2015, a University of Cincinnati team unexpectedly discovered a     
   large stone-built tomb of Late Helladic IIA date near Tholos Tomb IV on the 
   first day of renewed excavations at the Palace of Nestor, Pylos." [1]       
 • "Hundreds of artifacts of gold, silver, bronze, ivory, and semiprecious     
   stones were found with the body of a single male, 30–35 years old, dubbed   
   the 'Griffin Warrior.'" [1]                                                 
 • "Among the gold artifacts were four signet rings decorated with Minoan      
   ritual scenes." [1]                                                         
 • "we discuss the excavation of the grave, describe the rings, and consider   
   the significance of the rings’ iconography for the Mycenaeans who buried    
   them." [1]                                                                  
 • "In May 2015, a University of Cincinnati team unexpectedly discovered a     
   large stone-built tomb of Late Helladic IIA date near Tholos Tomb IV on the 
   first day of renewed excavations at the Palace of Nestor, Pylos." [2]       
 • "Hundreds of artifacts of gold, silver, bronze, ivory, and semiprecious     
   stones were found with the body of a single male, 30-35 years old, dubbed   
   the Griffin Warrior." [2]                                                   
 • "Among the gold artifacts were four signet rings decorated with Minoan      
   ritual scenes." [2]                                                         
 • "Here we discuss the excavation of the grave, describe the rings, and       
   consider the significance of the rings' iconography for the Mycenaeans who  
   buried them." [2]                                                           
 • "The grave dates to Late Helladic IIA, around 1450-1400 BCE, based on       
   associated pottery and grave goods." [2]                                    
 • "2016, Hesperia" [2]                                                        
 • "In May 2015 on the first day of renewed excavations at the palace, the team
   unexpectedly discovered a large, stone tomb. Hundreds of artifacts were     
   found along with the body of a single male, the 'Griffin Warrior.'" [3]     
 • "A plaque of ivory with a representation of a griffon in a rocky landscape  
   lay between the man’s legs." [3]                                            
 • "Among the gold artifacts were four signet rings decorated with Minoan      
   ritual scenes." [3]                                                         
 • "The Pylos Combat Agate, in our view a Cretan work of Late Minoan I, may be 
   the finest example of glyptic art yet discovered in a Minoan or Mycenaean   
   context. It was found in 2015 in the grave of the so-called Griffin Warrior 
   at Pylos." [3]                                                              
 • "It is no surprise then that the majority of the 1500 or more objects with  
   which he was buried are of Minoan style or Cretan manufacture." [3]         
 • "The face of the sealstone bears a representation of combat that draws on an
   iconography of battle scenes known from the Shaft-Grave period mainland and 
   Neopalatial Crete." [3]                                                     
 • "In May 2015, archaeologists Shari Stocker and Jack Davis from the          
   University of Cincinnati uncovered the pristine warrior’s grave near the    
   Palace of Nestor in Pylos." [4]                                             
 • "The body was that of a warrior in his mid-30s who died around 1500 B.C."   
   [4]                                                                         
 • "Buried with him were some 2,000 objects, including silver cups, beads made 
   of precious stones, ivory combs, a sword and four intricately decorated     
   solid gold rings." [4]                                                      
 • "They are made of multiple sheets of gold and depict very detailed scenes   
   and iconography straight out of Minoan mythology." [4]                      
 • "The rings probably come from Crete where they were used to place seals on  
   documents or objects." [4]                                                  
 • "We think that already in this period the people on the mainland already    
   understood much of the religious iconography on these rings, and they were  
   already buying into religious concepts on the island of Crete." [4]         
 • "These things clearly have a power connection...[and] fits with other       
   evidence that the elites on the mainland are increasingly closely connected 
   to the elites on Crete whether or not the rings were used in the Minoan way 
   for sealing objects." [4]                                                   
 • "the 3,500-year-old tomb of a Bronze Age warrior in southwest Greece" [5]   
 • "the so-called 'Griffin Warrior' revealed his most stunning historical      
   offering yet: an intricately carved gem, or sealstone" [5]                  
 • "The 'Pylos Combat Agate,' as the seal has come to be known" [5]            
 • "The seal is the latest and most significant treasure to emerge from the    
   treasure-laden tomb of the Griffin Warrior" [5]                             
 • "The limestone-encrusted sealstone was discovered lying face-down near the  
   right arm of the Griffin Warrior." [5]                                      
 • "Even more extraordinary... was that the meticulously carved combat scene   
   was painstakingly etched on a piece of hard stone measuring just 3.6        
   centimeters, or just over 1.4 inches, in length." [5]                       
 • "the discovery of four gold signet rings bearing highly detailed Minoan     
   iconography" [5]                                                            
 • "more than 3,000 objects arrayed on and around the warrior’s body" [5]      
 • The Griffin Warrior tomb was discovered in May 2015 near the Palace of      
   Nestor at Pylos during renewed University of Cincinnati excavations.        
   [1][2][3][4][5]                                                             
 • The tomb is dated to Late Helladic IIA, roughly 1450-1400 BCE, and contained
   the remains of a single male in his 30s described as a warrior. [1][2][4][5]
 • The burial included four gold signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual     
   scenes or iconography. [1][2][3][4][5]                                      
 • The rings are tentatively interpreted as probably originating in Crete and  
   as having been used to seal documents or objects. [4]                       
 • Researchers argue that the ring imagery and related Minoan grave goods      
   indicate substantial Mycenaean engagement with Minoan religious iconography 
   and cultural exchange. [1][2][3][4][5]                                      
 • The Pylos Combat Agate is described as a Cretan Late Minoan I sealstone     
   found in the Griffin Warrior tomb near the warrior's right arm, and its     
   combat scene draws on earlier mainland and Neopalatial Cretan iconography.  
   [3][5]                                                                      


